<div class="article-body">
    <Fragment set:html={`<h2>Why Use Grip Attachments</h2>
        <p>Grip attachments add difficulty to a dead hang without changing the exercise. Once a bodyweight hang is easy, extra difficulty (thicker bar, added load, one arm) gives more challenge than longer holds.</p>
        <p>A thicker bar makes it harder to close the fingers. An unstable surface asks more of the stabilizer muscles. A smaller hold puts the load on fewer fingers. Each tool creates new overload.</p>
        <p>Attachments also target specific weaknesses. Thick bars make the forearm flexors work harder. Fingerboards load the fingers on small edges. Rings add instability. Hand grippers train crushing grip. Dead hangs do not.</p>
        <p>None of these tools replace standard dead hangs. They supplement them. Keep standard-bar dead hangs as your primary grip exercise and add an attachment to 1 or 2 sessions a week. The sets, holds and loads on this page are common training practice and not a tested prescription.</p>

        <h2>Fat Grips and Bar Wraps</h2>
        <p>Rubber sleeves slide over a pull-up bar and increase its diameter from about 28 mm to 50 to 70 mm. Your fingers cannot close around the thicker surface.</p>
        <p>Thick bars make a hang harder. Holds are shorter. Nobody has tested whether the total training stimulus is equal for dead hangs. No source gives a multiplier for forearm activation in a dead hang.</p>

        <h3>How to Use Fat Grips</h3>
        <p>Slide the grips onto your bar. Grab the thickened bar with a full overhand grip. Hang until just before your grip gives out. Expect hang time to fall noticeably. Perform 3 to 4 sets with 90 to 120 seconds rest between sets.</p>
        <p>Start with one fat-grip session per week alongside your regular dead hang training. Add a second session if your forearms handle the first without soreness.</p>

        <h3>What to Look For</h3>
        <p>Choose grips made from high-density rubber that does not compress under hand pressure. The grip should fit snugly on a standard bar without sliding. Many models come in two sizes: standard (about 50 mm final diameter) and extreme (about 70 mm). Start with the standard size. Check the current price on Amazon UK. <a href="https://www.amazon.co.uk/s?k=fat+grip+attachments&tag=deadhangs-21" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Browse fat grip attachments on Amazon UK</a>.</p>

        <h2>Fingerboards and Hangboards</h2>
        <p>Fingerboards are wooden, resin or plastic boards with holds of varying depth, width and shape. Climbers use them to train the fingers on shallow edges. Mount a hangboard above a doorway and hang from specific hold sizes.</p>
        <p>Small holds load the fingers more than a full bar. The load moves from the palm and full hand to the fingertips.</p>

        <h3>Getting Started</h3>
        <p>Mount the hangboard above a doorway or on a wall-mounted bracket. Start on the largest holds. Hang for 10 to 20 seconds per set. Rest 3 minutes between sets. Perform 3 to 5 sets.</p>
        <p>Progress to smaller holds only after you can hang 20 seconds on the current hold size. Moving to a shallower hold too soon can overload the fingers.</p>

        <h3>Safety</h3>
        <p>Do not use a fingerboard until you can dead hang for about a minute on a standard bar. This is practical guidance and not a tested threshold. Warm up with finger extensions and wrist circles before every fingerboard session. Stop if you feel sharp pain in any finger joint.</p>
        <p>Wooden boards are often gentler on skin. Resin boards often offer more hold options. Check the current price on Amazon UK. <a href="https://www.amazon.co.uk/s?k=hangboard+fingerboard&tag=deadhangs-21" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Browse fingerboards on Amazon UK</a>.</p>

        <h2>Gymnastic Rings</h2>
        <p>Wooden or plastic rings hang from straps looped over a bar, beam or tree branch. Rings rotate freely in every direction. This instability makes your grip, forearms and shoulders stabilize constantly during a dead hang.</p>
        <p>A ring dead hang adds rotational instability that a fixed bar does not. This asks more of the shoulders and grip.</p>

        <h3>Ring Training for Dead Hangs</h3>
        <p>Loop ring straps over your pull-up bar or a sturdy overhead beam. Adjust strap length so the rings hang at arm's-reach height. Grip both rings with a neutral grip (palms facing each other) and hang.</p>
        <p>Expect hang time to fall compared to a bar. The instability tires your grip faster. Perform 3 to 4 sets of controlled ring hangs once a week.</p>
        <p>Rings also support <a href="/passive-vs-active-hang/">active hang</a> progressions, support holds, L-sits and muscle-up transitions. They are a versatile grip tool. Check the current price on Amazon UK. <a href="https://www.amazon.co.uk/s?k=gymnastic+rings&tag=deadhangs-21" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Browse gymnastic rings on Amazon UK</a>.</p>

        <h2>Hand Grippers</h2>
        <p>Spring-loaded grippers train crushing grip strength through a range of motion. Squeeze the handles together against calibrated resistance. Dead hangs train isometric grip endurance. Grippers train dynamic crush force. The two exercises complement each other.</p>
        <p>Use grippers between dead hang sessions. Perform 3 to 5 sets of 5 to 10 reps per hand with a gripper rated at 50 to 75% of your maximum single-rep effort. Rest 60 to 90 seconds between sets.</p>

        <h3>Choosing Resistance</h3>
        <p>Grippers come in fixed resistance ratings. Ratings are manufacturer figures and vary between brands. Buy a set of three: one you can close easily for warm-ups, one that challenges you for 8 to 10 reps and one you can barely close for 1 to 3 reps.</p>
        <p>Adjustable grippers let you set the resistance on a single tool and replace several fixed grippers. Check the current price on Amazon UK. <a href="https://www.amazon.co.uk/s?k=hand+grippers&tag=deadhangs-21" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Browse hand grippers on Amazon UK</a>.</p>

        <h2>Rice Bucket Training</h2>
        <p>Fill a bucket with dry rice. Plunge your hands to the wrist and perform gripping, opening, rotating and scooping movements against the resistance. This trains the hand extensors, wrist muscles and small hand muscles that dead hangs do not target.</p>

        <h3>Why Extensors Matter</h3>
        <p>Dead hangs train flexion: closing your hand around the bar. Extensors do the opposite and open your hand. Extensor work is commonly added to grip programs. No trial shows that rice bucket training prevents elbow tendinopathy.</p>

        <h3>Exercises</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Open and close:</strong> plunge hand into rice, squeeze a fistful, open hand fully, repeat for 20 to 30 reps</li>
          <li><strong>Wrist rotations:</strong> make a fist in the rice, rotate clockwise 15 times and then counter-clockwise 15 times</li>
          <li><strong>Finger spreads:</strong> push fingers into rice with hand open, spread fingers apart against resistance, 15 to 20 reps</li>
          <li><strong>Scoops:</strong> cup your hand, scoop rice upward against the weight of surrounding rice, 15 to 20 reps per hand</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A bucket and a bag of rice are all you need. Replace the rice if it gets damp or dusty.</p>

        <h2>Choosing the Right Tools</h2>
        <p>Match your tool selection to your training level. More tools do not mean faster progress. The wrong tool at the wrong time can add injury risk without much benefit.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Tool Recommendations by Level</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Beginner (under about a minute):</strong> No attachments needed. Focus on standard-bar dead hangs and <a href="/best-chalk/">chalk</a>. Build your baseline before adding complexity.</li>
            <li><strong>Intermediate (about 1 to 2 minutes):</strong> Add chalk and fat grips. One fat-grip session per week alongside 2 to 3 standard dead hang sessions. Rice bucket after each session for extensor work.</li>
            <li><strong>Advanced (over about 2 minutes):</strong> Add what you need. Hangboard for finger strength 1 to 2 times a week, rings for instability, fat grips for forearm overload, hand grippers for crushing strength and a rice bucket for extensor work.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The level cutoffs above are practical guidance and not norms.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Start with the simplest tool and add complexity only when your current stimulus stops producing gains. A bottle of chalk and a pair of fat grips serve many people for a long time before anything else is needed.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Do I need grip attachments for dead hangs?</h3>
          <p>Most beginners do not need attachments. Consider one when you can hang for about a minute and want more difficulty or want to target a weakness.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>What are fat grips and how do they help dead hangs?</h3>
          <p>Fat grips are rubber sleeves that increase bar diameter from about 28 mm to 50 to 70 mm. The thicker bar makes a hang harder. Holds are shorter. Nobody has tested whether the total training stimulus is equal for dead hangs.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Are fingerboards safe for dead hang training?</h3>
          <p>Fingerboards suit trained hands. Do not use them until you can dead hang for about a minute on a standard bar. Small holds load the fingers more than a full bar. Start with the largest holds and move to smaller edges gradually. Stop if you feel sharp pain in any finger joint.</p>
        </div>

